00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.920
These dinoflagellates didn’t come to us like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.640
spread out and diffuse across a slide. When&nbsp;
they first came to us, they were living a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:45.040
very comfortable life, tucked into the body&nbsp;
of a coral. So how did they end up here?

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:50.320
There’s a short answer and&nbsp;
a long answer to that question.&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.080
So let’s start with the short answer.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.160
James, our master of microscopes, recently&nbsp;
received a package from a coral farm in Germany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.360
We’ve explored some of the microscopic creatures&nbsp;
and bristle worms that were living and thriving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.945
in those packages in previous videos. But today&nbsp;
we’re here to focus on the main event: the corals.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.520
Unfortunately, it turns out that imaging corals&nbsp;
under the microscope is a little bit difficult.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.440
They don’t fit under the lens. So&nbsp;
instead of trying to squeeze them in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:24.880
James tried cutting thin slices out&nbsp;
of them that would be thin enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:32.880
to put under the microscope. But as he cut,&nbsp;
the corals began to bleed dinoflagellates.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.280
So that’s the short answer. The long&nbsp;
answer would take about 240 million years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.680
and we don’t have that kind of time. So let’s go&nbsp;
with something a little bit in between the two.&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.920
At particular spots in the ocean, where the&nbsp;
temperatures are just right, you can find coral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:58.720
reefs spread out like a bony but colorful garden.&nbsp;
The twists and turns of the corals within these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:05.760
reefs create spaces for other animals to swim&nbsp;
in and around, forming a stunning scenery that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:12.560
complements the incredible array of oceanic life.
Because of the forms they take on, corals look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.520
like plants. Even the ones from the coral&nbsp;
farm look like a weird, cute neon plant you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:25.440
might buy from a home decor store designed by Lisa&nbsp;
Frank. But corals are not plants. They’re animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.080
members of the phylum Cnidaria. Which&nbsp;
means that they are relatives of such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:35.600
former Journey to the Microcosmos stars&nbsp;
like hydra and the starlet sea anemone.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.880
And like their Cnidarian relatives, corals&nbsp;
have simple bodies. Through the middle of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:48.160
animal is a gastrovascular cavity that opens to a&nbsp;
mouth, lined with a ring of tentacles that sweep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.240
through the water in search of food.&nbsp;
To help them in their search for food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.360
are special cells on their tentacles called&nbsp;
cnidocytes, which are loaded with toxins.&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.880
The types of corals that we’re showing you today&nbsp;
are different from the types that assemble the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.640
vast majority of reefs. Those corals are&nbsp;
often called hard corals or stony corals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.280
thanks to the hard exteriors the corals make&nbsp;
for themselves out of calcium carbonate.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.200
But the corals that James looked at&nbsp;
under the microscope represent the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.760
softer side of their family.&nbsp;
Like this xenia, with its bluish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.200
arms moving with the water around it.&nbsp;
These soft corals still live in reefs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.760
but they don’t form the calcium carbonate&nbsp;
structures that their stony counterparts do.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.000
Many species of corals—stony and soft&nbsp;
alike—share another trait, the dinoflagellates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.840
though in this symbiotic context they’re&nbsp;
known by another name: zooxanthellae.&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.800
You can see their brown bodies&nbsp;
inside the soft coral Xenia.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.000
In some corals, the presence of these&nbsp;
zooxanthellae might not be immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.240
obvious. Like this zoanthus, which is under white&nbsp;
light. Those brown dots that were so immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:09.760
obvious when we zoomed in on the Xenia aren’t&nbsp;
so obvious now. So where are the zooxanthellae?

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.920
To find them, we can take advantage&nbsp;
of the photosynthetic pigment inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.640
of zooxanthellae: chlorophyll, which&nbsp;
glows red when it’s excited with red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.040
light. When we illuminate the coral with&nbsp;
this red light, we see the algae then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.480
as a bright red version of themselves&nbsp;
tucked into different parts of the coral.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:36.560
The oldest coral fossils that have been found&nbsp;
are around 400 million years old. However corals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.760
haven’t had algae living inside of them the whole&nbsp;
time. Now it’s hard to pin down the exact date this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:47.920
relationship began because dinoflagellate fossils&nbsp;
are hard to come by. But in 2016, a team of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.760
scientists studying coral fossils for the chemical&nbsp;
and physical evidence of zooxanthellae came up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:00.548
with a rough estimate for when the two may&nbsp;
have found each other: 210 million years ago.

00:05:02.125 --> 00:05:06.000
That would be around the late Triassic&nbsp;
period, a time when the first dinosaurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.120
were emerging. The waters that the corals&nbsp;
were living in weren’t particularly rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.240
in nutrients. And yet corals were able&nbsp;
to expand and diversify in those waters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.920
And they probably have the tiny algae living&nbsp;
inside of them to thank for that success.

00:05:22.445 --> 00:05:25.600
The corals either inherit&nbsp;
their algae or attract them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.200
releasing signals and communicating chemically&nbsp;
until they can essentially eat the algae.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.080
Except instead of eating, the corals&nbsp;
tuck them away, housing them in special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:41.520
compartments called symbiosomes within&nbsp;
the lining of their gastrovascular cavity.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:48.160
And from there, the exchange is simple but&nbsp;
powerful. The zooxanthellae live their algae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:52.880
lives within the coral, absorbing light&nbsp;
and photosynthesizing it into nutrients.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.040
But those nutrients aren’t&nbsp;
really for them. About 90%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:01.280
of what the zooxanthellae makes&nbsp;
gets sent to the coral instead.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.708
That’s a lot for the zooxanthellae to give up,&nbsp;
but the corals put that nutrition to good use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.600
Scientists studying stony corals found&nbsp;
that when the zooxanthellae were removed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.400
from the corals, or when it was darker and the&nbsp;
zooxanthellae couldn’t do their photosynthesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:22.400
the corals were much slower at making calcium&nbsp;
carbonate. And while there are corals that don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.760
form symbiotic relationships with zooxanthellae,&nbsp;
they’re also slower at making the calcium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:34.240
carbonate structures compared to symbiotic corals.
In exchange, the zooxanthellae get the protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.040
of their coral host. And they also&nbsp;
get the waste that the coral produces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:44.640
things like ammonia that are useless to the&nbsp;
coral but nutrition to the zooxanthellae.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:51.440
This exchange benefits far more than just the&nbsp;
coral and zooxanthellae. It’s also allowed many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:57.200
other animals to thrive as well. It’s estimated&nbsp;
that coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:04.560
floor, and yet they are home to around one third&nbsp;
of all marine species. Also, the overall effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.320
is beautiful, with zooxanthellae providing&nbsp;
much of the color we associate with corals.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:16.320
But it’s also a delicate relationship. When waters&nbsp;
become too warm or pollutants contaminate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:23.280
water, or any other myriad of things happen that&nbsp;
become too stressful for the corals, they expel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:29.440
their algae, losing their brilliant color in the&nbsp;
process in what’s known as “coral bleaching,”.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.720
And with our changing climate, these stresses&nbsp;
are starkly rendered in our oceans, as corals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:40.640
turn white and face new challenges without their&nbsp;
dinoflagellate inhabitants. The relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:45.760
they formed with zooxanthellae may have been&nbsp;
one of the defining features of their past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:51.474
but the loss of those relationships may be&nbsp;
one of the defining features of their present.

